# XSH sleep()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The text gives no upper bound on the suspension; the upper limits here
# are practical caps. Whether sleep() may return early is open (Q-14: the
# RATIONALE carries alarm()'s note on early alarms over to sleep()), so
# only XSH.sleep.002 depends on it; the others hold either way. Intervals
# are measured with CLOCK_MONOTONIC. Signals come from a child that
# repeats them, so that one sent before the call cannot leave the test
# waiting.

assertion  XSH.sleep.001
anchor     functions/sleep.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/sleep.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       sleep(2), with no signal, returns 0 after at least 1 s (and
           within a 12 s cap).
note       At least 1 s holds even under the reading that lets the
           wakeup come up to a second early (Q-14); XSH.sleep.002 is the
           strict bound.
test       t/001-returns-zero.c

assertion  XSH.sleep.002
anchor     functions/sleep.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
since      1988
says       sleep(1) does not return before 1 s has passed.
note       disputed (Q-14): the DESCRIPTION suspends the thread until the
           seconds have elapsed, but the RATIONALE says alarm()'s note on
           alarms that come up to a second early applies to sleep() too;
           a FAIL is logged as INSPECT.
test       t/002-not-early.c

assertion  XSH.sleep.003
anchor     functions/sleep.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/sleep.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       A caught signal ends sleep(10) early (before 9 s): it returns
           the unslept seconds, at least 10 minus the measured time in the
           call rounded down, and at most 10.
note       How a fraction of a second is rounded is not stated; any value
           between the rounded-down and rounded-up unslept time is taken.
test       t/003-interrupted.c

assertion  XSH.sleep.004
anchor     functions/sleep.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       A signal that is blocked, or ignored, when it arrives does not
           end sleep(2): it returns 0 after at least 1 s (Q-14 as in
           XSH.sleep.001); the blocked signal stays pending.
test       t/004-not-delivered.c

assertion  XSH.sleep.005
anchor     functions/sleep.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       In a process with a second thread, sleep(1) does not use
           SIGALRM: with alarm(100) pending and SIGALRM caught, it returns
           0, the handler does not run, the alarm is still pending
           (alarm(0) then returns 1 to 100) and the action of SIGALRM is
           unchanged.
test       t/005-threads-no-sigalrm.c
